  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nAn area of low pressure over Southern California is moving northeast \ntoday, which is tempering our high pressure just a bit to keep us a \nfew couple of degrees cooler this afternoon than yesterday. We'll \nstill be a bit above our seasonal norms though, back into upper 60s \nand low 70s in the eastern Magic Valley and lower Snake River Plain. \nWinds remain fairly light today, making for another beautiful fall \nafternoon to wrap up the weekend. \n\nTo start the week, the upper level low that will bring us moisture \neventually will be dropping south along the West Coast and our \nafternoon highs will still be in the 60s and low 70s with an \nincrease in wind gusts to around 20 to 25 mph in the afternoon and \nevening. As that low continues to drop southward, we will see it \npull a bit of moisture northward as early as Tuesday, but that \nprecipiation chance continues to look like isolated activity, at \nbest. That low makes its move eastward in the second half of the \nweek as troughing moves into the PNW on Friday. The combo of this \ntrough and low farther south will bring more widespread moisture and \na temperature drop for Thursday and Friday. While exact numbers of \nhow much precipitation we'll see is expected to vary greatly over \nthe next few days, there is decent agreement on temperatures falling \ninto the low and mid 60s on Thursday and 40s and 50s by Friday.",
